/**

@page page_exahype_design_philosophy Design philosophy and principles

\tableofcontents

ExaHyPE 2 is built as a layer on top of Peano 4 and the Peano 4 Technical Architecture. 
This C++ ExaHyPE 2 layer is built as a set of Peano 4 C++ utility files compiled into one shared library plus a Python API. 
It is relatively straightforward to understand by browsing through the C++ files, but it provides limited structure:
You find most of the compute ingredients of an ExaHyPE code in there, but the actual logic are not encoded in the C++.

For this, ExaHyPE 2 provides a Python API that allows users to describe their solver and its behaviour in a more abstract way.



## Python layer

### Architecture

@image html documentation/exahype2/design/python-architecture-layers.png

At the heart of ExaHyPE 2 are the solver classes and its aggregate. 
They model ***what*** is solved, i.e.~encode the physics.
Any implementation details how things are solved - either from a 
numerics point of view or from a performance point of view - are 
omitted from this description. This is the layer users interact with.

Each solver is tied to generators which determine ***which*** 
numerical scheme is employed.
They determine how the solver is mapped onto mesh entities, which
compute kernels are generated, and which solver steps make up one 
time step.
These generators map the solver description (see above) onto a task
graph of activities.

The task graph is then mapped onto Peano 4's action sets and traversals.
This part is already an element of the Peano core architecture. and is not specific to ExaHyPE 2.




### I/O and user interface conventions

- All steps report against the Peano logger. It enlists information through which 
  steps the generation runs through (but not what exactly has been produced). 
- The individual ingredients dump their specific information (which papers to cite, or 
  what actual solver/ingredient combination has been used) into the readme tied to the 
  Peano project. So the readme hosts information what has been done (cmp remarks on logger above).
- The individual code parts tend not to write any terminal output or at least keep it 
  very minimalist. This way, the user can write their own terminal output tailored towards their need.  
  
  
  


## Top-level ExaHyPE 2 layer

This layer is what most users will consider to be ExaHyPE 2. It is a thin 
Python package which allows a user to model a hyperbolic PDE solver. By
plugging many different objects together, users describe what their solver
will look like. In short, most ExaHyPE applications only create an instance
of exahype2.api.Project, and then they add instances of exahype2.api.Solver 
to it.

Once the structure of the solvers is in place, users attach generators to their
static solver description. Generators can be used to tailor all kinds of behaviour,
but, most importantly, the type of generator picked for a solver implies what 
numerical scheme you want to apply on a solver description.


## ExaHyPE'2 implememntation layer

The generators take the specification objects and they lower them into implementation
objects. This is where, for example, a Runge-Kutta scheme tells the implementation that
each time step consists of four substeps, which compute kernels we need to generate, and
so forth.

Once we have constructed this implementation representation, there's a second layer of
generators tied to the implementation:
They take the implementation representation and lower it into a Peano 4 representation.
On this level, idnividual solver steps are now for example mapped onto @ref glossary_events_and_action_sets "action sets" or traversals.


# Python design principles

@image html documentation/exahype2/architecture.png

At the heart of ExaHyPE2 are the solver classes and its aggregate.
Each solver class and each aggregate are tied to a generator class. 
The generator classes take the solver description and either lower them into a Peano 4 class or a plain C++ file tied to the present project.
Here, the same pattern repeats, i.e. Peano Python classes again hold generators which then ultimately lower the code in to plain C++.

1. The solver classes and their aggregates are purely ***descriptive***.
   That is, users build up these classes (instantiate them), set some attributes, attach them to each other, and so forth.
   They build up a representation what their solver features and can do.
   However, this is a description of the code bare of any information how these properties translate into code.
2. Each descriptive class holds a generator. 
   The generator knows how to translate what the class describes into functionality.
   This is where the ***how*** is encoded.
   By exchanging generators or writing their own ones, users can tailor a solver's behaviour.
3. A generator is allowed to do two things: yield a Peano 4 object, i.e. add something to the generated Peano 4 repository
   or generate plain C++ code. In the latter case, the Peano 4 project is informed about the source code, but nothing else.
   Where possible, the generator should ***not*** generate partial strings that are handed down into the Peano Python layer, 
   even though this sometimes cannot be avoided.


The aim of our architecture is to decouple implementation details (how) from mathematical modelling (what) that
inform the solver's numerical behaviour. For example, whether to use a GPU and whether to use task fusion
fall under implementation details and should be handled by a generator not the description. How many variables to use, which
PDE terms to enable and the patch size should be used however is a property of the model itself.

# Design patterns of larger ExaHyPE applications

Certain "design patterns" have proven of value when you design more massive 
codes on top of ExaHyPE 2.


## Narrow down the functionality

If you write a solver for a special domain, create a subclass of your ExaHyPE 
numerical scheme of choice. Users then shall work against this subclass. 

Next, you can narrow down the parameters. Do users really have to specify 
which terms are there if you already know the application domain and, therefore,
the PDE type? ExaHyPE solvers are not simple in the sense that they are very 
generic. It can not harm to narrow down options.

If users wanna extend the functionality of your bespoke solver later on, the 
classes are still full-blown ExaHyPE solvers. They can still add all kinds of
things they wanna add. 


## Keep functionality in C++

Unless you work with SymPy and similar options, it has paid off to move all
Physics into separate C++ header files. 
In this case, you can compile them independently, and the Python solvers 
only contain calls to these routines.
They map the solver-specific names for coordinates, for example, onto the 
right parameters for our C++ code.

This is obviously the right choice for developers favouring or needing 
Fortran:
Create some C++ bindings and let the Python stubs work against these stubs.

Peano's makefiles can be added "additional" C++ files besides all the glue
code Peano/ExaHyPE generate anyway. 
This way, you can embed your domain-specific realisations directly into the
overall build environment.


## Do not over-engineer

We have seen applications tremendously over-engineering code in the sense that
they try to cover for all possible usage scenarios: starting from parser 
options all the way down to solver features.
I do recommend that you go the other way round. 
After all, the easy modification and extension of code is definitely an 
advantage of Python.
ExaHyPE mirrors this: 
Nothing stops you form running N different solver on one mesh.

Decompose your bespoke solver again into elementary building blocks just as 
the ExaHyPE core does.


For general design patterns and idioms that recur across Peano and its extensions, see the
@ref page_developer_guidelines_home "developer guidelines and tips" page.

*/